WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:00.510
<v Speaker 1>Welcome.

2
00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:11.150
<v Speaker 1>Welcome back to our table as we open up the final pages of The Master and the Disciple and the really profound framework laid out by its author, Jafar ibn Mansur Ayaman.

3
00:00:11.230 --> 00:00:13.310
<v Speaker 2>Yeah, it's great to be back into this text.

4
00:00:13.390 --> 00:00:15.470
<v Speaker 1>This deep dive closes the dialogue.

5
00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:22.110
<v Speaker 1>The Disciple's 5 portion economy, the scholar's refusal, the trust held for the rightful master,

6
00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:24.750
<v Speaker 1>and a power whose cause has a name.

7
00:00:25.550 --> 00:00:30.030
<v Speaker 1>The purification portion is a trust for the rightful master until you meet him.

8
00:00:30.230 --> 00:00:32.870
<v Speaker 2>That is just, it's such a heavy place to start.

9
00:00:32.870 --> 00:00:33.550
<v Speaker 1>It really is.

10
00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:39.070
<v Speaker 1>I mean, we spend our entire lives acquiring resources, building our lives around what we can gather.

11
00:00:39.230 --> 00:00:45.470
<v Speaker 1>But today, we are closing this text with maybe the most terrifying question a seeker could possibly ask their teacher.

12
00:00:45.830 --> 00:00:47.630
<v Speaker 1>Like, how much of this is actually mine to keep?

13
00:00:48.110 --> 00:00:59.950
<v Speaker 2>And to understand how this completely upends our conventional concept of ownership, we first have to, well, anchor ourselves in the foundational teachings of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family.

14
00:01:00.110 --> 00:01:07.470
<v Speaker 2>Because up until this point in the narrative, the disciple has been carried through this towering intellectual architecture.

15
00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:10.750
<v Speaker 1>Yeah, exploring the union of the inner and the outer divine justice.

16
00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:13.390
<v Speaker 2>Intricate dream interpretation, all of it.

17
00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:19.230
<v Speaker 2>But eventually all of that abstract doctrine has to hit the hard ground of daily reality.

18
00:01:19.230 --> 00:01:23.310
<v Speaker 1>And there is no reality more grounding than what is literally sitting in your pocket.

19
00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:23.950
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

20
00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:29.630
<v Speaker 1>The narrative pivots from these lofty concepts to the most concrete situation imaginable.

21
00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:31.550
<v Speaker 1>The disciple looks at the scholar

22
00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:33.710
<v Speaker 1>and essentially asks for an audit.

23
00:01:33.710 --> 00:01:37.310
<v Speaker 2>He wants to know his actual physical obligations regarding his possessions.

24
00:01:37.550 --> 00:01:42.030
<v Speaker 1>So the scholar answers this by laying out a very clean, straightforward outer rule.

25
00:01:42.190 --> 00:01:44.750
<v Speaker 2>Yeah, he delivers it in these really distinct steps.

26
00:01:44.990 --> 00:01:47.470
<v Speaker 2>First, take only what was lawfully acquired.

27
00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:50.070
<v Speaker 1>Meaning you have to examine the supply chain of your wealth.

28
00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:50.750
<v Speaker 2>Precisely.

29
00:01:51.070 --> 00:01:53.710
<v Speaker 2>Ensure no one was exploited in the process of gaining it.

30
00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:56.910
<v Speaker 2>Second, pay the obligatory alms from the whole of it.

31
00:01:57.070 --> 00:01:57.470
<v Speaker 1>Okay.

32
00:01:57.470 --> 00:02:00.110
<v Speaker 2>Third, take a fifth from the finest portion.

33
00:02:00.110 --> 00:02:01.630
<v Speaker 1>Like the specific part of your wealth

34
00:02:01.750 --> 00:02:02.510
<v Speaker 1>you love the most.

35
00:02:02.990 --> 00:02:04.110
<v Speaker 2>Exactly that part.

36
00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:07.230
<v Speaker 2>Take 1/5 of that and spend it on those most deserving.

37
00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:14.830
<v Speaker 2>And finally, keep the remainder to spend on your family in your accustomed manner, giving any surplus to the needy.

38
00:02:15.070 --> 00:02:20.350
<v Speaker 1>It sounds like a basic ledger, but the scholar frames it as a literal physical purification.

39
00:02:21.070 --> 00:02:28.110
<v Speaker 1>He explains that you purify your possessions through charity in the exact same way you purify your physical body through virtuous acts.

40
00:02:28.270 --> 00:02:30.670
<v Speaker 2>The wealth itself requires A cleansing mechanism.

41
00:02:30.830 --> 00:02:33.950
<v Speaker 1>And that baseline is meticulously constructed from scripture.

42
00:02:34.350 --> 00:02:43.710
<v Speaker 1>We see this mapped out in the Quran, specifically in chapter 103 on repentance, which explicitly instructs that taking charity from wealth purifies it.

43
00:02:44.110 --> 00:02:45.790
<v Speaker 2>And causes it to increase.

44
00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:46.190
<v Speaker 1>Yes.

45
00:02:46.510 --> 00:02:48.830
<v Speaker 1>The scholar isn't inventing a new accounting method.

46
00:02:49.070 --> 00:02:54.350
<v Speaker 1>He is unfolding the reality that wealth, much like the physical body, accumulates A residue.

47
00:02:54.430 --> 00:02:58.590
<v Speaker 2>And A deliberate act of parting with it is the only mechanism that removes that residue.

48
00:02:58.590 --> 00:02:59.150
<v Speaker 1>Definitely.

49
00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:08.510
<v Speaker 1>And furthermore, verse 41 on the spoils of war establishes that the 5th belongs to the divine, to the messenger, and to specific categories like the near of kin and the needy.

50
00:03:08.670 --> 00:03:12.430
<v Speaker 2>Are we sure this isn't just making a simple charity rule overly complicated?

51
00:03:13.550 --> 00:03:15.470
<v Speaker 2>I mean, why not just give it all away?

52
00:03:16.270 --> 00:03:24.030
<v Speaker 2>If the ultimate goal of the seeker is detachment from the material world, why build this intricate mathematical structure to manage the money?

53
00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:30.190
<v Speaker 1>Because giving everything away blindly doesn't train the soul in the way it needs to be trained.

54
00:03:30.430 --> 00:03:31.070
<v Speaker 2>Really.

55
00:03:31.230 --> 00:03:32.750
<v Speaker 1>The structure is the pedagogy.

56
00:03:33.150 --> 00:03:36.990
<v Speaker 1>Emptying your pockets might make you poor, but it doesn't necessarily make you wise.

57
00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:38.030
<v Speaker 2>I see.

58
00:03:38.350 --> 00:03:44.510
<v Speaker 1>The scholar is taking a rule the disciple already knows in its outer form and forcing him to give it an inner form.

59
00:03:45.070 --> 00:03:46.670
<v Speaker 1>But here is where the narrative shifts.

60
00:03:46.670 --> 00:03:47.750
<v Speaker 2>The disciple's surprise.

61
00:03:47.870 --> 00:03:48.430
<v Speaker 1>Exactly.

62
00:03:48.590 --> 00:03:50.670
<v Speaker 1>The disciple surprises the scholar.

63
00:03:51.230 --> 00:03:53.150
<v Speaker 1>He hasn't just been passively listening.

64
00:03:53.390 --> 00:03:56.830
<v Speaker 1>He has essentially audited his own soul using his bank account.

65
00:03:57.150 --> 00:03:58.350
<v Speaker 2>Oh, that's fascinating.

66
00:03:58.350 --> 00:04:03.470
<v Speaker 1>And he reveals that he has already enacted an elaborate 5 portion spiritual economy of his own.

67
00:04:03.590 --> 00:04:11.710
<v Speaker 2>Let's examine what the disciple actually worked out, because it's a fascinating map of how a student learns to think organically rather than just repeating what he was told.

68
00:04:12.110 --> 00:04:14.790
<v Speaker 1>So portion one is exactly what the scholar asked for.

69
00:04:15.470 --> 00:04:19.150
<v Speaker 1>The standard obligatory purification, the basic alms.

70
00:04:20.030 --> 00:04:22.190
<v Speaker 1>But then the ledger becomes deeply psychological.

71
00:04:22.430 --> 00:04:29.070
<v Speaker 1>Portion 2 is set aside as an atonement for the harm he had done to his own soul before this dialogue even began.

72
00:04:29.070 --> 00:04:32.030
<v Speaker 2>And the brilliance of that second portion is its permanence.

73
00:04:32.110 --> 00:04:36.350
<v Speaker 2>He keeps it as a physical, tangible remembrance of his past sins.

74
00:04:36.350 --> 00:04:39.950
<v Speaker 1>Yeah, he explicitly states he does this to avoid complacency.

75
00:04:40.030 --> 00:04:42.670
<v Speaker 2>Think about how we usually handle guilt today.

76
00:04:43.070 --> 00:04:44.710
<v Speaker 2>We pay a parking ticket or make

77
00:04:44.830 --> 00:04:49.150
<v Speaker 2>quick reactionary donation, and we expect a clean conscience immediately.

78
00:04:49.230 --> 00:04:52.830
<v Speaker 1>We want the guilt to vanish the moment the money leaves our hands.

79
00:04:52.830 --> 00:04:53.550
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

80
00:04:53.790 --> 00:04:56.990
<v Speaker 2>But the disciple refuses to let himself off the hook that easily.

81
00:04:57.390 --> 00:05:01.710
<v Speaker 2>By holding onto this atonement portion, the memory of his fallibility stays visible.

82
00:05:01.790 --> 00:05:04.590
<v Speaker 1>He refuses to buy a clear conscience too cheaply.

83
00:05:04.830 --> 00:05:08.670
<v Speaker 1>He uses the unspent money as a daily confrontation with his own flaws.

84
00:05:08.830 --> 00:05:14.710
<v Speaker 2>Then we have portion 3, which he calls the gratitude portion, thanking the divine for the knowledge he has acquired.

85
00:05:15.070 --> 00:05:17.790
<v Speaker 1>But rather than spending it immediately to feel virtuous.

86
00:05:17.990 --> 00:05:19.310
<v Speaker 2>Which is what most of us would do.

87
00:05:19.390 --> 00:05:26.270
<v Speaker 1>He intends to hold it as a trust, untouched, until he reaches spiritual maturity and truly knows where it should be spent.

88
00:05:26.270 --> 00:05:28.510
<v Speaker 2>That requires immense discipline.

89
00:05:28.510 --> 00:05:33.870
<v Speaker 2>It is incredibly tempting to dispense with charity immediately to get that emotional gratification.

90
00:05:33.870 --> 00:05:35.670
<v Speaker 1>That rush of feeling like a benefactor.

91
00:05:35.670 --> 00:05:39.870
<v Speaker 2>Yeah, to hold it in trust means delaying that emotional payoff.

92
00:05:40.190 --> 00:05:44.710
<v Speaker 2>It means forcing yourself to be patient until you possess the wisdom to be an effective person.

93
00:05:44.830 --> 00:05:46.830
<v Speaker 2>giver rather than an impulsive one.

94
00:05:46.830 --> 00:05:53.550
<v Speaker 1>Then we get to portion 4, the portion designated for the needy among his brothers, and he insists on giving this hand to hand.

95
00:05:53.550 --> 00:05:54.510
<v Speaker 2>No intermediaries.

96
00:05:54.510 --> 00:05:54.910
<v Speaker 1>None.

97
00:05:55.070 --> 00:06:03.150
<v Speaker 1>No anonymity, which completely inverts how we normally think about philanthropy today, where anonymity is considered the ultimate moral flex.

98
00:06:03.230 --> 00:06:08.990
<v Speaker 2>We assume anonymous giving is superior because it protects the ego of the giver and prevents them from showing off.

99
00:06:09.070 --> 00:06:14.510
<v Speaker 1>But the disciple realizes that anonymous giving also strips the receiver of a human connection.

100
00:06:14.830 --> 00:06:27.150
<v Speaker 2>By handing it over himself, the disciple deliberately absorbs the awkwardness of being seen as the giver, but in exchange he grants the receiver the ultimate dignity of a face.

101
00:06:27.470 --> 00:06:31.470
<v Speaker 1>They are acknowledged as a human being, not just a line item on a charity sheet.

102
00:06:32.190 --> 00:06:36.990
<v Speaker 1>And this perfectly mirrors the profound wisdom found in the brilliant aphorisms.

103
00:06:36.990 --> 00:06:38.030
<v Speaker 2>Remind me of that one.

104
00:06:38.190 --> 00:06:43.630
<v Speaker 1>It's where the father of imams is reported to have said that the worth of every person is what they do well.

105
00:06:43.630 --> 00:06:44.350
<v Speaker 2>Oh yes.

106
00:06:44.590 --> 00:06:55.070
<v Speaker 1>The disciple hasn't even read that aphorism yet, but his own internal reflection led him to the exact same conclusion about the vast difference between a faceless transaction and a gift given face to face.

107
00:06:55.150 --> 00:06:57.070
<v Speaker 2>That nuance is just extraordinary.

108
00:06:57.070 --> 00:06:58.510
<v Speaker 2>And then there is the 5th portion.

109
00:06:58.510 --> 00:07:00.910
<v Speaker 1>The smallest, most personal cut of his wealth.

110
00:07:00.990 --> 00:07:01.910
<v Speaker 1>He saved this specific

111
00:07:01.910 --> 00:07:03.950
<v Speaker 1>specifically to support the scholar in his travels.

112
00:07:04.030 --> 00:07:08.030
<v Speaker 2>He wants to care for the lordly teacher who carried him to the edge of understanding.

113
00:07:08.030 --> 00:07:15.870
<v Speaker 1>So when you look at the entirety of this framework, we can see a 5 portion economy as five separable kinds of inner work, not 5 equal sums.

114
00:07:16.030 --> 00:07:20.750
<v Speaker 2>The amounts themselves don't matter nearly as much as the internal mechanisms they trigger.

115
00:07:21.470 --> 00:07:26.270
<v Speaker 2>Purification of the visible, remembrance of sin, gratitude held in trust,

116
00:07:26.590 --> 00:07:30.110
<v Speaker 2>generosity that grants dignity and love for the teacher.

117
00:07:30.190 --> 00:07:34.270
<v Speaker 1>He is using his wealth as a direct mirror for his spiritual progression.

118
00:07:34.510 --> 00:07:38.670
<v Speaker 2>But the scholar's reaction to hearing this plan is incredibly intense.

119
00:07:38.670 --> 00:07:39.550
<v Speaker 1>He weeps.

120
00:07:39.550 --> 00:07:39.990
<v Speaker 2>He weeps.

121
00:07:40.270 --> 00:07:42.910
<v Speaker 2>And then he gives a layered refusal.

122
00:07:42.910 --> 00:07:44.990
<v Speaker 2>He completely refuses his own portion.

123
00:07:45.470 --> 00:07:49.630
<v Speaker 2>He tells the disciple, quote, I have fled the conflicts and temptations of wealth.

124
00:07:49.790 --> 00:07:52.510
<v Speaker 1>This is a profound moment of modeling the tradition.

125
00:07:53.070 --> 00:08:00.270
<v Speaker 1>There is a deeply ingrained prophetic teaching that the upper hand is better than the lower hand, which receives.

126
00:08:00.510 --> 00:08:04.430
<v Speaker 2>The truest scholars operate as a shield against the material world.

127
00:08:05.150 --> 00:08:10.030
<v Speaker 2>They take from the world only what is absolutely necessary to survive and pass the rest on.

128
00:08:10.510 --> 00:08:14.110
<v Speaker 2>By refusing the money, the scholar is proving his authenticity to the student.

129
00:08:14.190 --> 00:08:15.230
<v Speaker 2>I don't buy that yet.

130
00:08:15.230 --> 00:08:18.990
<v Speaker 2>Why redirect the portion the disciples set aside for the scholar himself?

131
00:08:19.230 --> 00:08:26.750
<v Speaker 2>Rejecting the money to show personal detachment is one thing, but completely re-architecting the student's heartfelt gift seems like an overstep.

132
00:08:27.790 --> 00:08:30.350
<v Speaker 2>Why not just let the student learn from the act of offering it?

133
00:08:30.510 --> 00:08:35.870
<v Speaker 1>Well, because the scholar's role isn't just to be a passive recipient of the disciple's affection.

134
00:08:36.110 --> 00:08:40.510
<v Speaker 1>His role is to align the disciple with the ultimate reality of the tradition.

135
00:08:40.910 --> 00:08:42.670
<v Speaker 1>And he doesn't reject everything.

136
00:08:43.070 --> 00:08:46.350
<v Speaker 1>He actually validates the psychological work the student has done.

137
00:08:46.550 --> 00:08:49.710
<v Speaker 1>He tells them, yes, give to your brothers hand to hand so they see you.

138
00:08:49.870 --> 00:08:51.630
<v Speaker 2>He confirms the internal audit.

139
00:08:51.790 --> 00:08:52.270
<v Speaker 1>Yes.

140
00:08:52.350 --> 00:08:55.150
<v Speaker 1>Keep the atonement portion as a remembrance of your sins.

141
00:08:55.630 --> 00:08:59.230
<v Speaker 1>Yes, hold the gratitude portion in trust until you mature.

142
00:08:59.390 --> 00:09:00.990
<v Speaker 2>But the purification portion.

143
00:09:00.990 --> 00:09:04.590
<v Speaker 1>The very first portion the disciple thought he had figured out how to dispense.

144
00:09:05.390 --> 00:09:07.230
<v Speaker 1>The scholar entirely redirects it.

145
00:09:07.470 --> 00:09:10.590
<v Speaker 1>He tells him it is not the disciples to give to ordinary recipients.

146
00:09:10.990 --> 00:09:14.270
<v Speaker 1>The purification portion is a trust for the rightful master until you meet him.

147
00:09:14.350 --> 00:09:17.710
<v Speaker 2>That pivot is the structural core of this entire text.

148
00:09:17.950 --> 00:09:19.550
<v Speaker 2>And naturally the disciple asks,

149
00:09:19.870 --> 00:09:24.430
<v Speaker 2>who this noble person is, who is the rightful judge for whom this portion is held?

150
00:09:24.590 --> 00:09:27.310
<v Speaker 1>And the scholar introduces the architecture of trust.

151
00:09:27.590 --> 00:09:28.670
<v Speaker 1>He speaks of the Imam.

152
00:09:29.350 --> 00:09:31.710
<v Speaker 1>He describes this figure not just as a leader,

153
00:09:32.270 --> 00:09:34.510
<v Speaker 1>but as the spiritual judge of the age.

154
00:09:34.990 --> 00:09:39.390
<v Speaker 1>He says this is the one whose right has been made obligatory upon every believer.

155
00:09:39.390 --> 00:09:42.590
<v Speaker 2>The one who holds the keys to the heavenly gardens.

156
00:09:42.750 --> 00:09:46.430
<v Speaker 1>The guide whose palms reveal the light of the mountain of Sinai.

157
00:09:46.750 --> 00:09:53.710
<v Speaker 2>Daftree's historical work on Ismaili traditions, the Ismailis, points out something crucial here regarding that architecture.

158
00:09:54.030 --> 00:09:56.430
<v Speaker 2>This isn't just a metaphor for a wise king.

159
00:09:56.430 --> 00:09:57.550
<v Speaker 1>No, it's much deeper.

160
00:09:57.790 --> 00:10:02.150
<v Speaker 2>The prophet established that he was leaving behind two profound sources of guidance.

161
00:10:02.590 --> 00:10:08.590
<v Speaker 2>The book of the divine as the outer form and his family as the living continuation of the inner truth.

162
00:10:08.670 --> 00:10:09.790
<v Speaker 1>The 2 weighty things.

163
00:10:09.790 --> 00:10:10.590
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

164
00:10:10.910 --> 00:10:18.590
<v Speaker 2>So when the disciple thought he could dispense the purification portion based on his own personal judgment of who was needy, he was structurally mistaken.

165
00:10:18.670 --> 00:10:23.310
<v Speaker 1>That portion inherently belongs to the second of those two weighty things.

166
00:10:24.030 --> 00:10:28.430
<v Speaker 1>It must be held in trust for that living continuation of the inner truth.

167
00:10:28.830 --> 00:10:36.910
<v Speaker 2>It is an entire mechanism of trust that connects the physical coin in the disciple's hand directly to the highest spiritual authority on earth.

168
00:10:37.230 --> 00:10:41.310
<v Speaker 1>And this realization sets the final events of the narrative in motion.

169
00:10:42.350 --> 00:10:43.390
<v Speaker 1>The scholar departs.

170
00:10:43.470 --> 00:10:46.590
<v Speaker 2>He travels until he reaches his own spiritual father.

171
00:10:46.590 --> 00:10:47.470
<v Speaker 1>The elder master.

172
00:10:47.550 --> 00:10:47.870
<v Speaker 2>Yeah.

173
00:10:48.110 --> 00:10:53.070
<v Speaker 2>He narrates the disciple's story and the elder master recognizes the young man's immense qualities.

174
00:10:53.230 --> 00:10:55.230
<v Speaker 1>And he issues a very specific instruction.

175
00:10:55.230 --> 00:11:01.550
<v Speaker 1>Hurry back with this young man for I desire for him to become a gateway of loving kindness that may open for the people of his time.

176
00:11:01.550 --> 00:11:03.310
<v Speaker 2>We have to unpack that word gateway.

177
00:11:03.350 --> 00:11:04.190
<v Speaker 1>It's loaded.

178
00:11:04.350 --> 00:11:08.750
<v Speaker 2>Paul Walker explores this extensively in his work on early philosophical Shyism.

179
00:11:08.910 --> 00:11:11.830
<v Speaker 2>When the elder master says gateway, he is not just offering

180
00:11:12.030 --> 00:11:14.830
<v Speaker 2>a poetic compliment about the disciple having an open heart.

181
00:11:14.910 --> 00:11:16.670
<v Speaker 1>It is an institutional reality.

182
00:11:16.750 --> 00:11:17.230
<v Speaker 2>Yes.

183
00:11:17.310 --> 00:11:23.390
<v Speaker 2>When Walker describes this as a strict institutional office, he means the disciple is literally becoming the bottleneck.

184
00:11:23.550 --> 00:11:28.190
<v Speaker 1>He is the designated channel through whom spiritual instruction flows downward to the seekers.

185
00:11:28.190 --> 00:11:30.750
<v Speaker 2>And through whom their allegiance passes upward.

186
00:11:31.150 --> 00:11:32.590
<v Speaker 2>He's no longer just a student.

187
00:11:32.590 --> 00:11:35.630
<v Speaker 2>He is being promoted into the formal architecture of the tradition.

188
00:11:35.870 --> 00:11:38.190
<v Speaker 2>He is the active bridge for his generation.

189
00:11:38.910 --> 00:11:43.870
<v Speaker 1>But the physical toll of this promotion and the waiting involved is staggering.

190
00:11:44.350 --> 00:11:51.230
<v Speaker 1>The scholar hurries back just as instructed, but when the reunion happens, he finds the disciple radically transformed.

191
00:11:51.230 --> 00:11:52.270
<v Speaker 2>His body's weak.

192
00:11:52.430 --> 00:11:57.470
<v Speaker 1>His complexion is completely pale, and the source names the cause explicitly.

193
00:11:57.950 --> 00:12:01.870
<v Speaker 1>It is the consuming love a lover suffers in separation from the beloved.

194
00:12:01.950 --> 00:12:06.750
<v Speaker 2>That sounds like wordplay calling a body-changing, longing love instead of illness.

195
00:12:06.750 --> 00:12:08.790
<v Speaker 2>I mean, people get sick when their immune systems

196
00:12:08.990 --> 00:12:13.310
<v Speaker 2>fail or from sheer physical exhaustion, not because someone left town for a few weeks.

197
00:12:13.550 --> 00:12:18.830
<v Speaker 2>It feels like the text is romanticizing a physical fever to make a theological point.

198
00:12:18.990 --> 00:12:24.350
<v Speaker 1>It sounds like romanticizing a fever until you look at the tradition's deep grammar of separation.

199
00:12:24.990 --> 00:12:26.990
<v Speaker 1>Look at what happened to the scholar in chapter 2.

200
00:12:27.630 --> 00:12:28.670
<v Speaker 1>Remember his pallor?

201
00:12:28.830 --> 00:12:29.470
<v Speaker 2>Oh.

202
00:12:30.350 --> 00:12:31.590
<v Speaker 2>Oh, I concede the point.

